<h1>Is It Better to Have One Amazing Project or Five Decent Ones for Getting Hired?</h1>

<p class="answer-capsule">One amazing project wins every time. Recruiters spend seconds, not minutes, scanning portfolios, and a single piece of scored proof that hits a professional bar does what five half-finished pieces cannot: it answers "can this person do the job?" before the recruiter scrolls away.</p>

<div class="tldr">
  <ul>
    <li>Resume use <a href="https://www.testgorilla.com/skills-based-hiring/state-of-skills-based-hiring-2025/">fell to 67%</a> as skills-based hiring rises, meaning portfolio attention is even more compressed and competitive.</li>
    <li>Skills-based hiring now reaches <a href="https://www.testgorilla.com/skills-based-hiring/state-of-skills-based-hiring-2025/">85% of employers</a>, so proof of ability matters more than volume of credentials.</li>
    <li>Internship conversion sits at <a href="https://www.naceweb.org/">63.1%</a>, showing that evaluated experience carries weight in hiring decisions.</li>
    <li>A single project with recruiter-visible scoring collapses the "trust gap" faster than any breadth strategy.</li>
  </ul>
</div>

<section>
  <h2>Why does depth beat breadth in a recruiter's actual workflow?</h2>
  <p>Recruiters are not curators. They are triage nurses in a flood. A typical tech role draws hundreds of applications, and the first screen is not a careful review, it is a pattern-matching exercise to find reasons to stop reading. Five decent projects create five opportunities to spot mediocrity: incomplete documentation, generic scope, placeholder copy, a GitHub repo with three commits. One amazing project creates one moment where the recruiter pauses, scrolls back up, and actually thinks.</p>
  
  <p>The "amazing" threshold is not aesthetic polish. It is evidence that you operated at the level the role requires. Did you scope the problem yourself? Did you iterate after feedback? Did you ship something that had real constraints: a deadline, a user, a tradeoff you had to defend? Five projects that stop at "I followed the tutorial and deployed it" demonstrate attendance, not ability. One project that shows decision-making under uncertainty demonstrates the thing companies actually hire for.</p>
  
  <p>This is where the certificate economy misleads people. The implicit promise of course platforms and bootcamps is accumulation: more badges, more projects, more keywords, more coverage. But hiring is not a loyalty program. You do not level up by collecting items. You win by creating a single artifact so specific and so well-documented that a hiring manager can imagine you doing the job on their team tomorrow.</p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>What does "one amazing project" actually look like?</h2>
  <p>Depth has structure, not just hours logged. An amazing project has four layers that a decent project skips:</p>
  
  <p><strong>Real stakes.</strong> Not a Kaggle dataset with a leaderboard, but a problem where the wrong answer had a cost. Maybe you scraped and cleaned messy data because the clean version did not exist. Maybe you built for a user who was not yourself, and their feedback changed your scope. Maybe you hit a rate limit, a budget constraint, a conflict in requirements, and your documentation shows the pivot.</p>
  
  <p><strong>Visible reasoning.</strong> The code runs, but that is table stakes. What matters is the decision log: why this architecture, why not that one, what you tried and discarded. A README that walks a reader through three failed approaches before the working one is worth more than five repos with only final code. Recruiters hire people who think, not people who type.</p>
  
  <p><strong>Scored against a professional bar.</strong> This is the gap that zero closes. A project sitting in a personal GitHub is a claim. A project scored against what an actual company expects for that role is proof. The scoring is not a grade, it is a translation: this is how this work maps to what we need. Without that translation, the recruiter is left to guess, and guessing usually means "no."</p>
  
  <p><strong>Iteration on display.</strong> Version one was rough. Version three is what you ship. The amazing project shows the delta: feedback received, changes made, edge cases handled. Five decent projects that each stopped at version one show you can start things. One amazing project shows you can finish them, which is what you are being hired to do.</p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>When might breadth matter, and how little?</h2>
  <p>Breadth is almost always a trap. Early-career generalists applying to very small startups where "wear many hats" is literal might benefit from showing range: a backend endpoint, a frontend component, a data query, a user interview. Even then, two deep projects beat five shallow ones. The breadth should be a side effect of depth, not a substitute for it.</p>
  
  <p>The more common trap is the "T-shaped" self-description used to justify mediocrity in both depth and breadth. "I go deep in one area and have surface knowledge across many" sounds sophisticated. In practice it often means no project is strong enough to survive scrutiny. Pick the vertical that matches the role you want. Build one thing there that embarrasses you slightly in how much better it is than everything else you have made. Your portfolio is that project.</p>
  
  <p>Breadth is a hedge against not knowing what you want. Understandable early in a search, but not a strategy. It is procrastination with a nicer name. The person who commits to one domain, builds one exceptional piece of work, and routes all applications through that proof will outperform the generalist every time. The generalist forces every recruiter to do the work of figuring out where they fit. Why make them work harder to hire you?</p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>How does this connect to the broken university-to-job pipeline?</h2>
  <p>The filter operating system that runs education rewards breadth by design. You take twelve courses a semester, pass twelve exams, accumulate twelve credits. No single course needs to be exceptional; the system only checks that you survived the curve. The result is graduates with transcripts full of B-pluses and portfolios full of "completed assignments."</p>
  
  <p>Companies do not operate on credit hours. They operate on specific problems that need specific solutions in specific timeframes. One amazing project is how you signal that you understand this difference. You are not asking to be evaluated by the filter's rules, where volume and endurance substitute for quality. You are submitting proof that you can operate under the rules that actually govern work.</p>
  
  <p>The fix is not more of the same. It is not five more decent projects that look like coursework. It is one piece of work so clearly professional that it renders the rest of your portfolio irrelevant, making the recruiter stop scrolling and move you to the next stage. That is the entire game.</p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>What should you do if you currently have five decent projects and no amazing one?</h2>
  <p>Consolidate, do not accumulate. Pick the project with the most honest stakes, the one where something real was on the line, and rebuild it to a professional standard. Document the decisions. Add the feedback loop. Score it against a bar that matters. Delete or deprioritize the rest. Your portfolio is not a museum; it is an argument, and arguments are stronger with one decisive piece of evidence than with five weak ones.</p>
  
  <p>The sunk cost is painful. You spent weekends on those other projects. But recruiters do not award points for effort. They award interviews for clarity. One amazing project gives them a reason to say yes. Five decent ones give them five reasons to keep looking.</p>
</section>

<section class="faq">
  <h2>FAQ</h2>
  
  <div class="faq-item">
    <h3>Should I include group projects in my "one amazing" pick?</h3>
    <p>Yes, if you can isolate your contribution with precision. Group work is real work, but ambiguity kills. Label exactly what you built, what decision you owned, what feedback you incorporated. Never let a recruiter wonder whether you were the driver or the passenger.</p>
  </div>
  
  <div class="faq-item">
    <h3>How long should an amazing project take to build?</h3>
    <p>Longer than a weekend, shorter than a year. Most strong projects that score at a professional bar represent 80-150 hours of focused work, often spread across 4-8 weeks with real iteration. The time is less important than the density of decisions documented.</p>
  </div>
  
  <div class="faq-item">
    <h3>Does the project need to be in the exact tech stack of the role?</h3>
    <p>Close enough to map, not identical. A React project for a Vue role is fine if you can articulate component architecture decisions that transfer. A Python data pipeline for a Go backend role is harder. The test is whether the hiring manager can see you thinking in their domain, not whether you memorized their tools.</p>
  </div>
  
  <div class="faq-item">
    <h3>What if I am a career switcher with no relevant projects yet?</h3>
    <p>Build one before you apply widely. The temptation is to spray five half-done projects across applications to show momentum. Resist it. One project built to the standard of the new role, with visible reasoning and ideally scored feedback, outperforms any volume of unfinished claims. The switch is already a credibility gap; do not widen it with thin proof.</p>
  </div>
  
  <div class="faq-item">
    <h3>How does zero help build this kind of project?</h3>
    <p>Zero provides company-shaped scenarios with real constraints, not tutorial walkthroughs. You work on problems scoped to actual roles, receive per-submission feedback against a professional bar, and build scored proof that recruiters can access directly. The output is one exceptional piece of work, not a collection of certificates.</p>
  </div>
</section>

